{
  "clarification": "---
name: clarify-mesh-changes
description: Establish which region of a 3D model a person means and what change they want. Use visual references when words such as this part, around the eyes, bigger, or rounder leave materially different edits possible.
---

# Clarify mesh changes

Use ordinary language and the visible model to establish a shared reference. Use a highlight to visually ground region-specific edits and named parts. Follow clear requests, including undo and selections from choices you offered. Ask for clarification when the intended region or action is ambiguous; do not require a special confirmation phrase.

## Identify the region

If the spoken request includes a \`cursor-at-start-of-spoken-request\` reference, use that image and region for its pointing words. It was requested at the first transcript chunk, not at exact audio onset; compare its request and capture times. Do not replace it with wherever the cursor moved later. If it failed or is ambiguous, ask for a new reference. Otherwise, when the person refers to the region they are pointing at, call \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\` to ask the open viewer for a fresh capture. Cursor mesh vision is blue; agent-proposed regions are teal. A captured result contains an image, capture time, pointed part, world-space Gaussian sphere, and model identity. An editable-model hit also includes \`region.modelLocalBlob\`: pass it as one blob to \`viewer_highlight\`, with \`identity.design.path\`, \`revision\`, and \`url\`, to show your own proposed region. Do not move the camera before taking the snapshot. The capture occurs when the viewer processes the request, through an immediate viewer wakeup; it does not recover where the cursor was when the person spoke. On \`no_hit\`, ask them to keep the pointer over the intended surface and retry. On \`viewer_unavailable\`, ask them to open the 3D preview. On \`stale_view\` or \`capture_failed\`, resolve the viewer issue and capture again; never substitute an earlier region. A snapshot does not approve an edit.

Read \`mesh_state\` and inspect the selected model's source before choosing coordinates. Whenever you refer to a part such as a chin, jaw, cheek, eye, rim, or crease, first show that part with \`viewer_highlight\`. Explain it as the teal highlighted area with a simple positional description. Do not assume the person knows anatomical or modelling terms. For several distinct parts, highlight and discuss one at a time; a combined highlight is appropriate only when the person is choosing the combined edit region. Named parts can help identify a region, but one mesh may contain many meaningful features. Describe left and right from the person's current view or use an unambiguous feature; do not silently switch between the wearer's and viewer's perspective.

For an editable model loaded with \`viewer_load\`, call \`viewer_highlight\` with the exact \`design.path\`, \`design.revision\`, and \`design.url\` from \`mesh_state\`. Give the region a short descriptive label. Supply a few ellipsoidal blobs with \`center\`, \`radii\`, and \`rotationDegrees\`, each a three-number array. Coordinates are millimetres in the model's local frame, after part transforms but before whole-design placement. Rotation uses XYZ Euler degrees. Radii specify nominal extent, not diameter or Gaussian standard deviation. The viewer blends their influence with a fixed soft fade and reveals wireframe within the region.

Start with the smallest collection that covers the intended area. A flattened, tilted blob can cover an eyebrow; two blobs can indicate both eyes without covering the entire forehead. Inspect depth as well as width and height: a blob can also intersect the back of a thin shell. This is an approximate conversational region, not a precise edit mask or a selection of entire connected parts.

The tool waits for a matching receipt from the visible viewer. Only \`rendered\` confirms rendering; \`not_rendered\` or \`superseded\` does not. On failure, explain that the highlight is not confirmed, ask the person to open the preview, and retry. Never say “I have highlighted it” before a \`rendered\` result. Rendering still does not establish that the person saw or approved it. Use their reply in context, including corrections or a choice you offered. On \`stale_model\`, read the state again and recompute against the new source; resolve build errors first. Highlights are temporary and clear when the source revision changes or another model loads. Imported STL objects do not support this tool.

## Establish the change

Ask one short question in the conversation about the uncertainty that matters, then wait for a spoken or typed reply. Do not use a questionnaire or require clicking an option. For an eye region, distinguish enlarging the openings from widening the surrounding material. Establish whether bigger means wider, taller, thicker, or more raised when the context does not settle it. Do not infer exact edit boundaries from the soft highlight.

When the person corrects the region, stop work based on the previous region, replace the complete blob list, and clarify any remaining ambiguity. Keep their requested change in context. Do not make unrelated improvements while waiting. Hiding the highlight is a viewing preference, not approval. Confirmation of a region is not authorization for an unrelated change or a print.

When the requested change is clear, edit the model source and inspect the resulting preview and measurements. Clear an obsolete reference with \`blobs: []\`, using the current model identity and an empty label. Explain changes using the person's feature names rather than coordinates or triangle indices. Only refer to labels or viewer controls that actually exist; do not invent hover labels.
",
  "cursor": "Ask the open 3D viewer to capture the current cursor Gaussian region and a PNG of the current view. Cursor mesh vision is blue; agent viewer_highlight regions are teal. Captures when the viewer services this request, not when the person last spoke. Prefer an attached cursor-at-start-of-spoken-request reference for that utterance. Returns the pointed object/part, world-space point and radius in millimetres, model identity, camera and capture time. Editable-model hits include modelLocalBlob ready for viewer_highlight with identity.design path/revision/url. Read the image and region before highlighting or editing. no_hit means no active cursor intersection; viewer_unavailable means no response from an open viewer. Never substitute an earlier hover or treat a soft region as an exact edit mask.",
  "highlight": "Replace temporary Gaussian wireframe regions on the editable model loaded by viewer_load. Read mesh_state for design.path, revision and url; inspect model source to locate regions. Coordinates are model-local millimetres before viewer_transform, including part transforms. Rotation uses XYZ Euler degrees. Up to eight ellipsoids combine by maximum influence, with a fixed soft fade. All intersected surfaces, including the back, may be highlighted. Empty blobs clears. Does not edit geometry or exports. Waits for the visible viewer to acknowledge the matching highlight. Returns rendered only after acknowledgment; not_rendered or superseded is not success. Rendering is not user confirmation. Use this tool to visually ground region-specific edits and explanations of named parts. Follow the user’s requested action and clarify ambiguous intent; no special confirmation phrase is required. Use clarify-mesh-changes for guidance.",
  "instructions": "### Project type

This project is for designing and creating 3d prints.

### Project plugin

The primary plugin to enable that is print-studio, a Cordis plugin.

The plugin provides the ability to design, iteratively, the 3d mesh through its accompanying renderer.

The plugin provides the ability to prep and print the mesh to the user's personal 3D printer.

### Editable designs

Create and organize independent models in \`models/<name>/model.js\`, with each model's helpers beside it. The viewer automatically discovers these folders and shows model-selection controls. Call \`viewer_load\` with a model's path to bring it into view; use \`mesh_state\` to see the available models and current selection. Keep existing models when adding another.

Each entry exports synchronous \`build(THREE)\` returning a fresh Mesh or Group. Use millimetres and Z up. Edits to \`.js\` and \`.mjs\` files under the entry directory reload automatically while the preview is open. Relative imports need file extensions; pass THREE into helpers instead of importing a package. Keep named parts and adjustable dimensions in source. Do not modify the viewer or create cameras, lights, controls, DOM elements, timers, or background work in the model.

Use \`viewer_transform\` for whole-design placement and \`viewer_camera\` for the viewing angle. Read \`mesh_state\` for build errors and measurements. A failed edit keeps the last successful preview; fix the source to recover. Preview checks do not establish print readiness.
",
  "load": "Select and show a project JavaScript model in the 3D viewer. Organize independent models as models/<name>/model.js; the viewer discovers them automatically. Export synchronous build(THREE) returning a Mesh or Group. Use millimetres and Z up. Relative .js/.mjs imports inside the entry file’s folder are supported; pass THREE to helpers. The visible viewer automatically reloads source edits. The viewer owns camera, lighting and controls; do not edit its implementation. Code executes in the renderer: keep builds synchronous and free of DOM access, timers and other side effects. Read mesh_state for build errors and measurements after the viewer loads it. Loading does not certify print readiness.",
  "state": "Read the 3D preview scene without changing it: every object, its placement, and the measurements of its composed mesh. Includes discovered models and the selected model. Saved JavaScript models reload when the viewer opens; STL scene objects remain in memory. Read this tool instead of assuming what is on screen.",
}
